# spec: Conversational Router and Pair Programmer

Use for `/spec <intent>`, any development task, feature request, troubleshooting or engineering question; route each intent to its delivery workflow. The spec constitution (`devkit get-context spec`) defines the axes, workflows and approval boundaries.

## Development approach

Before SDD routing, read `uv run devkit config show`; under `adaptive-dev`, route ordinary work as the constitution's
opening paragraph says (the `/adaptive-dev` entry when selected). An unreadable policy stops policy-dependent work; never
guess or switch it. The rest of this skill applies to SDD.

## Constraints

- If `devkit spec policy` fails, stop policy-dependent work.
- Under `FEEDBACK_POLICY reminder=contextual`, suggest an optional `/spec-feedback` sweep when completed work produced reusable observations (not for routine completions or wording edits); it never gates completion or merge.

## Route the intent

1. **Playbook discovery at intake**, before classification and before any Unit, so a selected playbook's depth and scope inform the classification. Intake is a `/spec <intent>` or a user's own message requesting a change or operation outside the agreed scope of the current task; no message from another agent (Host payload, worker turn, nested role), no hook and no stage `devkit workflow next` names is intake. An explicit `/deep-research` invocation is not intake either: it gets no playbook discovery, classification or Unit and follows its own skill; work the research later informs starts as ordinary intake. Task kind and planning depth never exempt a task. Skip discovery for:

   | Skip case |
   | :--- |
   | Continuation of an existing proposal or Unit: `/spec-next`, a worker turn, a Host resuming a handoff, the stage `devkit workflow next` names |
   | A follow-up within the same task: answers, clarifications, review fixes, retries, corrections of the delivered change ("use option b", "rerun the tests") |
   | The entry is already explicit: the user names a playbook ID, or a failing rule's remediation names one |
   | Execution inside a selected playbook: a step that asks for a change gets no nested search |
   | An information-only question that requests no change and no operation ("what does `devkit sync` write?") |

   Also skip every later intake once the user said not to use playbooks in this conversation; never offer a candidate the user declined again in the same conversation, even for a reworded request. Not skipped: a new request after earlier work finished, even inside a Planning or Development conversation ("now clean up open feedback"), and a question asking how to perform an operation ("how do I adopt devkit here?").

   Run one `devkit playbook search "<English query>" --limit 3 --max-bytes 4096` per intake (deliberately overriding the project's `[playbooks]` settings). Write the query in English as the requested operation on its artifact, with the canonical artifact nouns (ADR, feedback, Living Spec, proposal, playbook, rule, release) in place of the user's paraphrase and without qualifiers that only name the subject ("write a playbook for changelog checks", not "write a release playbook for changelog checks"); no automatic refinement follows. Scores never decide. Offer a candidate only when all hold:
   - **operation, not topic**: its `description` names the operation the request asks you to perform on the artifact; building, fixing or changing the tool, code or documents about that artifact is a topic match and is never offered ("Feedback Quick Fixes" never offers `feedback-hygiene`);
   - **primary operation**: it covers the request's main operation, not one step of a larger change;
   - **scope fits**: its scope does not exceed the requested scope ("refactor the sync module" never offers a repository-wide review playbook);
   - **one line**: at most one offer line per intake.

   Name a passing candidate in that line with step 2's classification statement; nothing of it runs before the user confirms, and the confirmation is its explicit invocation. End the intake reply with the offer and wait for the answer before creating a Unit, a branch or an edit. On a match read [playbooks](references/playbooks.md); only on a match. No match continues the ordinary path silently, with no question and no draft offer. A search error (`NOT_SYNCHRONIZED`, `CONFIG_LOCAL_RENDER_STALE`, `ARGUMENT_INVALID`, `SEARCH_EXTRA_MISSING`, a lock timeout) counts as no match: never run its remediation for discovery's sake; report it in one line only when it also blocks the task.
2. Classify per the constitution and [classification](references/classification.md); state the classification and activity effort ([effort](references/effort.md)). Ask only when goal, scope or constraints are unresolved.
3. Before creating a Unit or making the first edit, read [git policy](references/git-policy.md) and settle the branch: in a worktree per [worktree](references/worktree.md), otherwise per [branches](references/branches.md).
4. Start the workflow:

| Classification | Workflow | Start |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `session` | `sdd-session-<task_kind>` | Agree the intent in the conversation; no records. |
| `overall-refactor` | `sdd-overall-refactor` | `direct` only, with the user; no Unit. |
| `spec` | `sdd-spec-<task_kind>` | On `dev-<slug>`: `devkit spec new <slug> --title "..." --summary "..."` (`--workflow sdd-spec-bugfix` for a defect), record `plan-intent`, commit. |
| `proposal` | `sdd-proposal`, then `sdd-spec-<task_kind>` | Backlog: `devkit spec proposal new <slug> --task-kind feature\|bugfix`; judge `size` against the `XL` definition ([backlog](references/backlog.md)), show it with `planning_effort` in the plan-intent request, then record `plan-intent`. Planning on `plan-<slug>` per [planning](references/planning.md), or a direct acceptance ([backlog](references/backlog.md)). Development on `dev-<slug>` from `devkit spec proposal handoff <slug>`. |
| Spike, independent review, or reconcile verification in an isolated context | Support role | `spec-delegate`, as the `activity-effort` asks. |
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}| Native approval preparation, or integration after CLI/PR `dev-integration` authorization | Integration | `spec-integrate`. |
{% else %}| Integration after separately existing CLI/PR `dev-integration` authorization | Integration | `spec-integrate`. |
{% endif %}
A workflow's rules are in `.devkit/managed/spec/workflows/<workflow-id>.md`, its stages in `devkit workflow show <workflow-id>` (`--unit <dir>` once a Unit exists). Go back within Development with `devkit workflow return --unit <dir> --to <stage>`; Development never returns to Planning. Record envelopes per [envelope](references/envelope.md) and a run's questions and problems per [worker records](references/worker-records.md).

## Completion

1. Approaching review or completion, read [completion](references/completion.md).
2. Before `dev-integration`, run the `collect-feedback` sweep the workflow names (`spec-feedback`), unless `[STAGE_POLICY: collect-feedback state=off]`.
3. Present the final change report in the [envelope](references/envelope.md) format, then integrate with `spec-integrate`.

- Before a Planning run returns, follow [per-run feedback](../spec-feedback/references/sweep.md#planning-run-coverage).
- Before deleting a proposal without handoff, follow [feedback retirement](references/feedback-retirement.md).

Done when the final change report is presented and code review, commit and integration are each reported with its authority.
